 ## Overview
-Chunked Volume Rendering is a memory-management technique for image-order volume rendering that processes rays in fixed-size batches (chunks) to bound GPU memory usage during inference [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of out-of-memory errors when rendering high-resolution images or large ray bundles by decoupling the total ray count from the per-forward-pass memory footprint [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Sparse Ray Marching (Training)](3890-sparse-ray-marching-training.md), which optimizes sampling density during training, and [Inference Ray Compositing](3790-inference-ray-compositing.md), which focuses on the compositing logic itself; Chunked Volume Rendering specifically addresses the *batching* of the entire ray processing pipeline [S1, unofficial].
+Chunked Volume Rendering is a memory-management technique for image-order volume rendering that processes rays in fixed-size batches (chunks) to bound GPU memory usage during inference [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of exceeding available VRAM when rendering high-resolution images or large ray bundles by decoupling the total ray count from the per-forward-pass memory footprint [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Sparse Ray Marching (Training)](3890-sparse-ray-marching-training.md), which optimizes sampling density during training, and [Inference Ray Compositing](3790-inference-ray-compositing.md), which focuses on the compositing logic itself; Chunked Volume Rendering specifically addresses the *batching* of the entire ray processing pipeline [S1, unofficial].
